        {
          "id": "SP_CHAETOPTERUS_PUGAPORCINUS_OSBORN_ROUSE_GOFFREDI_ROBISON_2007",
          "name": "Chaetopterus pugaporcinus Osborn, Rouse, Goffredi & Robison, 2007",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Chaetopterus",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "<p>Anterior Region: composed of 9 segments, compressed so notopodia project under prostomium, forming an arch running dorsoventrally. Each notopodium has 6-10 lanceolate chaetae. There are neuropodial uncini on either side of the middorsal ciliated groove. The prostomium is large (to 20% of body length), bilobed, and folded towards the posterior. Peristomium is broadly horseshoe-shaped, with short grooved palps. <em>C. pugaporcinus </em>has no eyes.</p> <p>Middle Region: composed of 5 segments, with one segment that is greatly enlarged, accounting for more than 80% of body length, and as broad as the animal is long. The segments give the worm a nearly spherical appearance when undisturbed. When disturbed, it contracts, withdrawing the anterior and middle regions toward the body center. There are neuropodial uncinal plates on the posterior part of some segments, and notopodia in other segments with up to 10 internal, simple chaetae lateral to the uncinal plates.</p> <p>Posterior Region: difficult to discern from pygidium but seems to consists of 1 uniramous segment. The pygidium consists of a cylindrical appendage immediately dorsal to the anus. The worm is semitransparent white to beige with dark brown to purple pigment lining interior of buccal region. The gut is packed with dark material, making it easily distinguishable through its body wall. No glandular plate, ventral shield, or plastron is found.",
